% DBS: this course's labs are in two languages and overwhelmingly in one.
% Almost every listing is SQL; Python appears only in the measurement
% harnesses, and bash only to start the sqlite3 shell.  E-Commerce needed
% five lexers because extending a storefront genuinely takes five languages;
% a database course does not, and pretending otherwise would be padding.
%
% The slide pipeline reads the language= option off each lstlisting and maps
% it to a Pygments lexer, so each name here must be one Pygments also knows:
% sql, python, bash.  Spelling drift between the two ends is the defect to
% watch for; the algorithms course shipped lab cards reading "73 lines of C"
% because "C++" lost its plus signs on the way.
%
% A lab listing is always pure ASCII: listings has no Unicode support under
% pdfLaTeX, so a stray arrow or non-breaking space is a compile error, not a
% typo.  Keep lines at or under 76 columns -- wider than that and the code
% panel on the slide has to shrink below the 10.5 pt floor.  checklabs.py
% enforces both, and accepts "// --- n." as a step marker as well as "# --- n."
% and "-- --- n." for SQL.
%
% listings ships SQL, Python and bash, and this course needs no others.  All
% three are used exactly as listings ships them, with NO derived dialect.
%
% A derived dialect is a trap here, and it cost the E-Commerce build a day:
% listings matches language names case-insensitively, so
% \lstdefinelanguage{SqlDBS}[]{SQL} followed by \lstalias{sql}{SqlDBS} makes
% "sql" resolve to a dialect whose base is itself, and the first listing
% exhausts the TeX input stack with an error that names neither the file nor
% the language.  Keeping the stock names also keeps them identical to the
% Pygments lexer names the slide pipeline asks for, which is the thing most
% worth having.
%
% SQLite's dialect is close enough to listings' generic SQL that the
% highlighting is right without help.  The keywords it does NOT know --
% PRAGMA, STRICT, AUTOINCREMENT, WITHOUT ROWID, RETURNING, VACUUM -- are
% added below rather than left unhighlighted, because several of them are
% the point of the chapter they appear in.
\lstset{
  morekeywords=[1]{PRAGMA,STRICT,AUTOINCREMENT,RETURNING,VACUUM,ANALYZE,
                   ATTACH,DETACH,REINDEX,SAVEPOINT,RELEASE,DEFERRED,
                   IMMEDIATE,EXCLUSIVE,CONFLICT,ROLLBACK,ABORT,REPLACE,
                   GENERATED,ALWAYS,STORED,VIRTUAL,RECURSIVE,WINDOW,
                   OVER,PARTITION,FILTER,EXCLUDE,TIES,EXPLAIN},
}

